    "id": "old-spec/rev20-01",
    "question": "<p>(a) Express <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\frac{2}{(r+2)(r+4)}\"></span> in partial fractions.</p><p><span class=\"marks\">(1)</span></p><p>(b) Hence show that</p><div class=\"equation\" data-display=\"true\" data-math=\"\\sum_{r=1}^{n} \\frac{2}{(r+2)(r+4)}=\\frac{n(7n+25)}{12(n+3)(n+4)}\"></div><p><span class=\"marks\">(5)</span></p>",
    "steps": [
      "<p>Subtracting the two unit fractions gives numerator <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(r+4)-(r+2)=2\"></span>. Their indices differ by two, so each negative fraction cancels with the positive fraction two steps later. That is why two terms survive at each end.</p>",
      "<p>(a) <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\dfrac2{(r+2)(r+4)}=\\dfrac1{r+2}-\\dfrac1{r+4}\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>(b) The sum begins <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(1/3-1/5)+(1/4-1/6)+(1/5-1/7)+\\cdots\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Cancelling two places apart leaves <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"1/3+1/4-1/(n+3)-1/(n+4)\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>Over <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"12(n+3)(n+4)\"></span> the numerator is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"7(n+3)(n+4)-12(2n+7)=n(7n+25)\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>"
    ],
    "markSchemeSteps": [
      "<p>Retained exercise: 6 marks.</p>",
      "<figure class=\"exam-solution-source\" style=\"margin:2mm 0\"><img src=\"resources/old-spec/rev20-01-ms-01.webp\" alt=\"Source marking table for 2014 · Q1, extract 1\" style=\"display:block;margin:auto;height:auto;object-fit:contain;width:100%;max-height:210mm\"></figure>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "old-spec/rev20-02",
    "question": "<p>(a) Show that <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"r^{2}(r+1)^{2}-(r-1)^{2}r^{2}\\equiv 4r^{3}\"></span></p><p><span class=\"marks\">(3)</span></p><p>Given that <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\sum_{r=1}^{n}r=\\frac{1}{2}n(n+1)\"></span></p><p>(b) use the identity in (a) and the method of differences to show that</p><div class=\"equation\" data-display=\"true\" data-math=\"(1^{3}+2^{3}+3^{3}+\\ldots+n^{3})=(1+2+3+\\ldots+n)^{2}\"></div><p><span class=\"marks\">(4)</span></p>",
    "steps": [
      "<p>Extract <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"r^2\"></span> before expanding the two squares. Their difference factors as <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"2\\cdot2r\"></span>. When summing, the expression <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"r^2(r+1)^2\"></span> agrees with the negative term at the next index, leaving endpoint products only.</p>",
      "<p>(a) Factor out <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"r^2\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>The bracket is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(r+1)^2-(r-1)^2=[(r+1)-(r-1)][(r+1)+(r-1)]=4r\"></span>, giving <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"4r^3\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>(b) Summing the differences, the two adjacent terms at every interior index cancel. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>The surviving terms give <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"4\\sum r^3=n^2(n+1)^2-0\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Thus <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\sum r^3=[n(n+1)/2]^2=(1+2+\\cdots+n)^2\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>"
    ],
    "markSchemeSteps": [
      "<p>Retained exercise: 7 marks.</p>",
      "<figure class=\"exam-solution-source\" style=\"margin:2mm 0\"><img src=\"resources/old-spec/rev20-02-ms-01.webp\" alt=\"Source marking table for 2015 · Q4, extract 1\" style=\"display:block;margin:auto;height:auto;object-fit:contain;width:100%;max-height:210mm\"></figure>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "old-spec/rev20-03",
    "question": "<p>(a) Show that, for <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"r &gt; 0\"></span></p><div class=\"equation\" data-display=\"true\" data-math=\"r-3+\\frac{1}{r+1}-\\frac{1}{r+2}=\\frac{r^{3}-7r-5}{(r+1)(r+2)}\"></div><p><span class=\"marks\">(2)</span></p><p>(b) Hence prove, using the method of differences, that</p><div class=\"equation\" data-display=\"true\" data-math=\"\\sum_{r=1}^{n} \\frac{r^{3}-7r-5}{(r+1)(r+2)}=\\frac{n(n^{2}+an+b)}{2(n+2)}\"></div><p>where a and b are constants to be found.\n<span class=\"marks\">(5)</span></p>",
    "steps": [
      "<p>The polynomial term <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"r-3\"></span> needs its ordinary arithmetic sum, while the remainder is a difference of consecutive reciprocals. The last negative fraction is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"-1/(n+2)\"></span>. Combining its contribution with the polynomial sum over <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"2(n+2)\"></span> keeps the end correction visible.</p>",
      "<p>(a) The numerator over <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(r+1)(r+2)\"></span> is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(r-3)(r+1)(r+2)+1\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Expand to get <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"r^3-7r-5\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>(b) Sum <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"r-3\"></span> to obtain <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"n(n+1)/2-3n\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>The remaining differences begin <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(1/2-1/3)+(1/3-1/4)\"></span> and cancel to <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"1/2-1/(n+2)\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>Over <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"2(n+2)\"></span> the combined numerator is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"n(n-5)(n+2)+n=n(n^2-3n-9)\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Thus <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"a=-3,\\ b=-9\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>"
    ],
    "markSchemeSteps": [
      "<p>Retained exercise: 7 marks.</p>",
      "<figure class=\"exam-solution-source\" style=\"margin:2mm 0\"><img src=\"resources/old-spec/rev20-03-ms-01.webp\" alt=\"Source marking table for 2016 · Q2, extract 1\" style=\"display:block;margin:auto;height:auto;object-fit:contain;width:100%;max-height:210mm\"></figure>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "old-spec/rev20-04",
    "question": "<p>(a) Show that, for <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"r &gt; 0\"></span></p><div class=\"equation\" data-display=\"true\" data-math=\"\\frac{1}{r^{2}}-\\frac{1}{(r+1)^{2}}\\equiv \\frac{2r+1}{r^{2}(r+1)^{2}}\"></div><p><span class=\"marks\">(1)</span></p><p>(b) Hence prove that, for <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"n \\in \\mathbb{N}\"></span></p><div class=\"equation\" data-display=\"true\" data-math=\"\\sum_{r=1}^{n}\\frac{2r+1}{r^{2}(r+1)^{2}}=\\frac{n(n+2)}{(n+1)^{2}}\"></div><p><span class=\"marks\">(3)</span></p><p>(c) Show that, for <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"n \\in \\mathbb{N}, n &gt; 1\"></span></p><div class=\"equation\" data-display=\"true\" data-math=\"\\sum_{r=n}^{3n}\\frac{6r+3}{r^{2}(r+1)^{2}}=\\frac{a n^{2}+b n+c}{n^{2}(3 n+1)^{2}}\"></div><p>where a, b and c are constants to be found.</p><p><span class=\"marks\">(3)</span></p>",
    "steps": [
      "<p>The equality <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(r+1)^2-r^2=2r+1\"></span> explains how to recognise the desired difference of reciprocal squares. Write down the first and last differences before cancellation. For a shifted range substitute its actual endpoints into those surviving fractions.</p>",
      "<p>(a) The numerator over <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"r^2(r+1)^2\"></span> is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(r+1)^2-r^2=2r+1\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>(b) The sum is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(1-1/4)+(1/4-1/9)+\\cdots+(1/n^2-1/(n+1)^2)\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Cancelling gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"1-1/(n+1)^2=n(n+2)/(n+1)^2\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>(c) The numerator is three times that in (a). The surviving endpoints are <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"3[1/n^2-1/(3n+1)^2]\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>Expanding gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(24n^2+18n+3)/[n^2(3n+1)^2]\"></span>, so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(a,b,c)=(24,18,3)\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>"
    ],
    "markSchemeSteps": [
      "<p>Retained exercise: 7 marks.</p>",
      "<figure class=\"exam-solution-source\" style=\"margin:2mm 0\"><img src=\"resources/old-spec/rev20-04-ms-01.webp\" alt=\"Source marking table for 2017 · Q1, extract 1\" style=\"display:block;margin:auto;height:auto;object-fit:contain;width:100%;max-height:210mm\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"exam-solution-source\" style=\"margin:2mm 0\"><img src=\"resources/old-spec/rev20-04-ms-02.webp\" alt=\"Source marking table for 2017 · Q1, extract 2\" style=\"display:block;margin:auto;height:auto;object-fit:contain;width:100%;max-height:210mm\"></figure>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "old-spec/rev20-05",
    "question": "<p>(a) Express <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\frac{1}{(r+3)(r+4)}\"></span> in partial fractions.</p><p><span class=\"marks\">(1)</span></p><p>(b) Hence, using the method of differences, show that</p><div class=\"equation\" data-display=\"true\" data-math=\"\\sum_{r=1}^{n} \\frac{1}{(r+3)(r+4)}=\\frac{n}{a(n+a)}\"></div><p>where a is a constant to be found.</p><p><span class=\"marks\">(5)</span></p><p>(c) Find the exact value of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\sum_{r=15}^{30} \\frac{1}{(r+3)(r+4)}\"></span></p><p><span class=\"marks\">(2)</span></p>",
    "steps": [
      "<p>The first positive term is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"1/4\"></span> because the index starts at one, and the final negative term is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"-1/(n+4)\"></span>. When evaluating a tail, cancel directly between its new endpoints or subtract two complete partial sums; both leave the same boundary fractions.</p>",
      "<p>(a) The partial fractions are <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"1/(r+3)-1/(r+4)\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>(b) The first terms are <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(1/4-1/5)+(1/5-1/6)\"></span> and the last is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"1/(n+3)-1/(n+4)\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>All interior terms cancel. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>The sum is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"1/4-1/(n+4)=n/[4(n+4)]\"></span>, so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"a=4\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>(c) Summing from 15 to 30 leaves <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"1/18-1/34=4/153\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>"
    ],
    "markSchemeSteps": [
      "<p>Retained exercise: 8 marks.</p>",
